# Self-Contained Prompt: Prove or Correct the A021 RFDE Invariant-Graph Theorem

You are acting as a specialist in retarded functional differential equations, normally hyperbolic invariant manifolds for semiflows on Banach spaces, exponential dichotomies, graph transforms, and Lyapunov–Perron methods.

Your task is to produce a publication-grade proof—or a rigorous diagnosis showing why the proposed result cannot be proved without stronger hypotheses—for the RFDE invariant-graph claim stated below.

Do not rely on informal appeals to finite-dimensional Fenichel theory. Do not assume that a bounded set of continuous histories is compact. Do not call a set normally hyperbolic merely because one subsystem has a stable equilibrium. Every invoked persistence theorem must be identified precisely and its hypotheses matched one by one.

---

## 1. Mathematical setting

Fix a delay \(\tau>0\). Let

\[
\mathcal X=C([ -\tau,0],\mathbb R^{m}),
\qquad
\mathcal Y=C([ -\tau,0],\mathbb R^{n}),
\]

with the supremum norm unless you explicitly replace these spaces with another justified phase space.

For a trajectory \(x\), write

\[
x_t(\theta)=x(t+\theta),
\qquad \theta\in[-\tau,0],
\]

and similarly for \(y_t\).

Consider the weakly coupled RFDE

\[
\dot x(t)
=F(x_t)+\varepsilon_c f(x_t,y_t),
\]

\[
\dot y(t)
=G(y_t)+\varepsilon_c g(x_t,y_t),
\tag{1}
\]

where

\[
F:\mathcal X\to\mathbb R^m,
\qquad
G:\mathcal Y\to\mathbb R^n,
\]

and

\[
f:\mathcal X\times\mathcal Y\to\mathbb R^m,
\qquad
g:\mathcal X\times\mathcal Y\to\mathbb R^n.
\]

Assume the maps have whatever \(C^r\), boundedness, and local-Lipschitz properties your selected theorem genuinely requires. State those properties explicitly; do not hide them in “regularity assumptions.”

The coupling size comes from a smooth Liebig yield gap:

\[
\varepsilon_c
=C e^{-\rho\Delta_y}
+\varepsilon_{\mathrm{phys}},
\tag{2}
\]

where \(\rho>0\) is soft-minimum sharpness, \(\Delta_y>0\) is a uniform yield gap, and \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{phys}}\ge0\) bounds an additional physical coupling.

The soft-minimum calculation supplies

\[
\pi_j
\le
w_{\min}^{-1}e^{-\rho\Delta_y}
\qquad(j\ne k),
\]

so off-limiting service derivatives are \(O(e^{-\rho\Delta_y})\). You may take this estimate as given.

At \(\varepsilon_c=0\), system (1) is the product system

\[
\dot x(t)=F(x_t),
\qquad
\dot y(t)=G(y_t).
\tag{3}
\]

Assume \(G\) has an equilibrium \(y_*\in\mathbb R^n\). Let \(\widehat y_*\in\mathcal Y\) denote the corresponding constant history.

