## 2. The proposed result that needs repair

The intended conclusion is that, for sufficiently small \(\varepsilon_c\), the coupled RFDE possesses an attracting invariant graph over the binding \(x\)-dynamics:

\[
\mathcal M_{\varepsilon_c}
=
\{(\phi,h_{\varepsilon_c}(\phi)):
\phi\in\mathcal A_x\},
\]

where

\[
h_{\varepsilon_c}:\mathcal A_x\to\mathcal Y,
\qquad
h_0(\phi)=\widehat y_*.
\]

Here \(\mathcal A_x\subset\mathcal X\) must be a precisely defined compact invariant base, compact invariant manifold, or bounded-geometry invariant manifold for the uncoupled binding semiflow. It may not be replaced by the generally noncompact set

\[
C([ -\tau,0],K_x)
\]

for a compact finite-dimensional set \(K_x\).

The desired invariance identity must be written in history-space notation. If \(\Phi_{\varepsilon_c}^t\) is the coupled semiflow and \(\pi_x,\pi_y\) are coordinate projections, require

\[
\pi_y\Phi_{\varepsilon_c}^t
(\phi,h_{\varepsilon_c}(\phi))
=
h_{\varepsilon_c}
\left(
\pi_x\Phi_{\varepsilon_c}^t
(\phi,h_{\varepsilon_c}(\phi))
\right)
\tag{4}
\]

whenever the trajectory remains in the chart/domain.

Do not write \(h(x_\tau)\) when \(h\) is defined on histories. Use one consistent object: \(h_{\varepsilon_c}(x_t)\).

The desired attraction estimate is of the form

\[
\operatorname{dist}
\left(
\Phi_{\varepsilon_c}^t(\phi,\psi),
\mathcal M_{\varepsilon_c}
\right)
\le
C e^{-\beta' t}
\operatorname{dist}
\left((\phi,\psi),\mathcal M_{\varepsilon_c}\right),
\tag{5}
\]

for initial histories in a specified neighborhood and some \(\beta'>0\).

