## 5. Non-negotiable safeguards

- Do not assume `bounded` implies `compact` in an infinite-dimensional history space.
- Do not call the product set normally hyperbolic without tangent/normal domination.
- Do not cite finite-dimensional Fenichel as though it automatically applies to RFDEs.
- Do not use \(h(x_\tau)\) when \(h\) maps histories.
- Do not infer a global graph from a local theorem.
- Do not infer all-time tracking from a compact finite-time estimate.
- Do not transfer a Hopf theorem unless the complete center/normal spectrum and crossing hypotheses are verified.
- Do not conceal a missing theorem behind “standard arguments.”
- If a proof is unavailable, explicitly return a corrected conjecture and the strongest rigorously proved finite-time result.

The preferred outcome is correctness and a minimal defensible theorem, not preservation of the original claim’s strength.